# Line-ending policy for this repository.
#
# Without this file, every checkout is at the mercy of the checking-out machine's
# `core.autocrlf`. Git for Windows defaults that to `true`, so a contributor who has
# never touched it inherits a checkout where every first-party source file is
# rewritten from this repo's LF to CRLF — and the corruption does not announce
# itself as a line-ending problem. Measured on `josh-home` while validating #144 and
# #149: `pint --test` reported six fixers against code with no style violations, and
# `tests/Unit/CiParityTest.php` failed six of seven assertions, because its
# line-based parsing of `ci.yml` and `local-ci.mjs` was matching against a trailing
# `\r` that should never have been there. Neither failure described the code.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` moves that decision from the machine to the repository: Git
# normalizes to LF in the object database on commit, and checks out LF everywhere
# regardless of `core.autocrlf`. See #152 and `docs/DECISIONS.md` D-066.
#
# `text=auto` detects binary content and leaves it alone, so this is safe as a
# blanket rule; the explicit entries below exist so the intent is readable rather
# than inferred, and so a new binary type is a deliberate line rather than a
# silent reliance on detection.
* text=auto eol=lf

# First-party source, stated explicitly. These are the files the defect was
# measured against.
*.php   text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.xml   text eol=lf
*.stub  text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf
*.neon  text eol=lf
*.dist  text eol=lf

# A vendor patch is whitespace-sensitive and `patches.lock.json` pins its sha256,
# so a checkout that rewrote its line endings would fail to apply or to verify.
*.patch text eol=lf

# File types that arrive with the shared skills sync from `uams-claude-skills`
# (#362): `.claude/sync-manifest.jsonc` and the skill templates, the release-note
# generator and its test, and the Cursor rule copies under `.cursor/rules/`.
# They are synced, not edited here, but they are tracked here, so they need a
# stated policy like any other text file. See #368.
*.jsonc text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
*.mdc   text eol=lf

# Dotfiles carry no extension a glob can match, so they are named literally.
.gitattributes         text eol=lf
.gitignore             text eol=lf
.git-blame-ignore-revs text eol=lf
.editorconfig          text eol=lf

# Binary: never normalized, never diffed as text.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary

# The decision log is APPEND-ONLY at its tail, so two tickets resolving
# concurrently always collide there and neither branch is wrong: git sees two
# continuations of the same last line and cannot choose between them. The
# resolution is always concatenation, never judgement, which is exactly what the
# built-in `union` driver does. Four such collisions were absorbed across the
# estate in about ninety minutes on 2026-09-14. See #246 and D-075.
#
# Union appends ours-then-theirs and does NOT order the result, so it can land a
# lower D-number after a higher one. `scripts/check-decision-log-order.mjs` is
# what catches that, and the two halves are useless apart: the driver removes the
# conflict, the guard catches what the driver got wrong. The same seam also
# drops the blank line between the two entries it stitches together, and the
# guard reports that too (#260).
#
# WHERE THIS APPLIES: to a merge git performs locally -- the author-side sync
# `.claude/rules/sync-pr-branch.md` requires before the gating run. GitHub's
# merge does not run a driver out of a repository's `.gitattributes`, so a
# decision-log branch pushed unsynced is refused on the forge exactly as if this
# line were absent. Measured in the sibling repository on the identical pair:
# driver present, local merge clean; line removed, conflict; GitHub, dirty.
# See #262 and D-076. The sync is the step this line serves, not one it waives.
#
# And it is read from the tree of the checkout PERFORMING the merge on every modern
# git measured (2.39.5, 2.53, 2.54; git 2.32 differs), so a branch cut before this line
# landed conflicts once on its first sync. The exit code of a decision-log sync is
# therefore not a signal; `scripts/check-decision-log-order.mjs` is. See #277, D-076.
#
# This ADDS to the `*.md text eol=lf` rule above rather than replacing it --
# attributes accumulate, and the later, more specific line only sets what it
# names. Verified with `git check-attr text eol merge -- docs/DECISIONS.md`.
docs/DECISIONS.md merge=union

# What a GitHub download of this plugin holds (#439). A deploy tool installs the
# plugin from `git archive` of a tag, which carries every tracked file unless a
# line here leaves it out; a server serving the plugin folder for every file
# type but PHP then publishes the agent folders, docs, tests and tool config.
# A keep-list rather than a deny-list, per the `keep-the-download-to-what-runs`
# rule: the first two lines leave out every top-level entry, dot-entries
# included, and each line after them puts back one path the plugin runs. A new
# runtime path needs its line here in the same change;
# `.claude/skills/download-archive-check/check-download-archive.mjs`, the
# `download-archive` job in `local-ci.json`, is what notices a new non-runtime
# one. `config/local.php` is git-ignored, so it never reaches the archive.
/* export-ignore
/.* export-ignore
/uamswp-migration-api.php -export-ignore
/includes -export-ignore
/config -export-ignore

# The license ships in the GitHub download (#378). Under the keep-list #439
# added, which leaves out every top-level entry and puts back only what the
# plugin runs, `LICENSE` would be left out too; GPL-2.0 section 1 asks that a
# copy of the license go with each copy distributed. Must stay below the
# keep-list's `/* export-ignore` above, since a later matching line wins.
/LICENSE -export-ignore
